WASHINGTON -- U.S. Rep. Bill Owens, a Democrat who gained national attention when he won a special election against a Tea Party candidate in 2009, said today he will not seek a third term representing Northern New York.
"After careful thought and consideration, I have decided not to seek re-election for the 21st Congressional District this November," Owens said in a statement. "I have enjoyed the opportunity to travel the district, meeting and serving the families and business owners of this vast community. It has truly been a privilege to serve, and I plan on continuing to work for a brighter future for the region."
Owens, D-Plattsburgh, did not offer specific reasons for his retirement other than to say he wants to "undertake new endeavors and spend more time with my family."
"My appreciation for the support of my wife, children, grandchildren and close friends cannot be sufficiently expressed in words," Owens said. "There are others -- too numerous to list -- to whom I owe a great debt of gratitude."
Owens was the first Democrat to be elected since the Civil War-era in a North Country district that includes Jefferson and St. Lawrence counties. He had slim margins of victory when he was re-elected in 2010 and 2012.
Several Republicans have expressed interest in challenging Owens this year, including Elise Stefanik, who worked in the administration of former President George W. Bush and ran debate preparations for Rep. Paul Ryan, R-Wisc., during his 2012 campaign for vice president.
