North Korea seems absolutely determined to develop the ability to strike the United States with nuclear weapons. Donald Trump has promised the American people that he would never let that happen. Unless either Kim Jong-Un or Donald Trump blinks, the end result of this standoff could be a catastrophic nuclear war, with the potential loss of life in the millions.
Russia and China have proposed a deal where North Korea freezes their nuclear weapons and missile tests in exchange for the U.S. and South Korea terminating their joint military exercises. The Trump administration has rejected this proposal outright with U.N. Ambassador Nikki Haley referring to it as ��insulting��.
Want to see editorial cartoons on the topic?
Follow us on Flipboard.
While North Korea may not negotiate in a serious manner, at the very least we should give diplomacy a try. The Russia-China double freeze proposal seems to be the most promising means of defusing what is developing into a very dangerous situation.
Loren Spiekerman
Foster City
U.S. should consider North Korea offer
North Korea wants to negotiate, but the United States does not want to negotiate. North Korea would like to have a peace treaty with South Korea, but this is off the table. My guess is that North Korea would give up its nuclear and ICBM weapons programs if the U.S. would end its occupation of the Korean peninsula.
Is the above prescription too preposterous to negotiate in order to have peace on the Peninsula rather than the destruction of both North Korea and South Korea (and loss of U.S. military forces stationed in South Korea)? I think naught.
A journey begins with one step. The United States, North Korea and South Korea need to take that step.
Larry Dorshkind
Redwood City
Democrats, if you stay on your current path, you will not win Congress in 2018.
In my classrooms, I find that students and parents are ignorant of history. Democrats need to remind voters of their accomplishments: the 40-hour week, unemployment insurance, a minimum wage, Equal Pay Act, the Civil Rights Acts, environmental protections and the Affordable Care Act.
And their plans: commitment to Social Security, Medicare and improvement to the Affordable Care Act, a living wage, climate accords, and a strong defense with bold diplomacy.
Running against Trump is a woefully insufficient strategy. His unpopularity does not translate into support for Democrats.
Spell your agenda out. Make it simple. Use it in town halls, interviews and public statements. The Democratic message of 2016 election did none of this.
Trump is not running in 2018. More than 450 Republicans are. Don��t squander your chance.
Pat Andrews
Political Science Instructor
West Valley College
