A sort of gold-rush mentality has taken root among many technology executives in Connecticut -- at least among the few who have time to contemplate their good fortune.
However, many spend most of their waking hours meeting demand for new products or services, or searching for scarce workers.
"We work on Internet time here," said Tom Shea, chief operating officer of the Internet start-up company Sirocco Systems of Wallingford.
Connecticut currently has a growing number of companies such as Sirocco that are staking out potentially lucrative claims upon the vast expanse of the World Wide Web, the source of the greatest economic potential of the late 20th century.
That trend should boost the state's technology sector, which in recent years has posted some of the highest rates of revenue and employment growth in the state.
Yet two other major events will fuel the growth among information technology companies this year -- an increase in demand from the expected signing this fall of a $1 billion contract to upgrade the state's computer systems and the global stretch run to correct so- called Y2K flaws before Jan. 1.
"There will be significant opportunities for local companies," said Laura Kent, executive director of the Connecticut Technology Council.
There have been significant gains in the state's technology sector, particularly the broad sector known as information technology.
According to the state Department of Labor, employment in software and information technology industries has more than doubled in the decade; they now employ about 23,000 workers.
"There's a great base of talent in Connecticut that has yet to be tapped," noted Michael Centrella, president and chief executive of Bethel-based Merlot Communications.
Centrella and Chuck McCormack formed Merlot last year after selling their first company, Avidia of Wallingford, for $94 million -- just a year after they founded it.
Merlot is selling products that will provide local area network service, high-speed Internet access and phone service on a single platform.
The rise of the Internet as a business force is driving the creation of many new small companies, Centrella said.
"It's no different than other breakthroughs.There is a lot of heat around it because it allows you to do innovative things, much like when personal computers came on the scene," Centrella said.
Sirocco Systems, for instance, has no product. Yet even before it moved into its headquarters the company attracted $2 million in seed money. In his first week with the company, Shea said, he received six calls from venture capitalists clamoring to give Sirocco more money.
The investors are betting that Shea and his partner, Jonathan Reeves, will make Internet tools that allow businesses to ship and receive more information more quickly and efficiently than they can with existing technology.
Investors are willing to place big bets on the partners because in 1998 -- just 18 months and $11 million in funding after they started Sahara Networks in Wallingford -- they sold the company in a stock deal valued at $212 million.
There are a number of Connecticut firms trying to duplicate that success.
"In this practice, at least, it is clear that the growth rate of software companies in Connecticut is simply phenomenal," said Tim Reilly, partner in charge of the Connecticut technology practice at PricewaterhouseCoopers.
One firm that hit it big in 1998 was Cyberian Outpost. The company sells computer-related products on-line, and became one of Wall Street's Internet darlings of 1998.
Operating from tiny Kent, Cyberian went public last summer, and its stock price had almost doubled, to about $35 a share, by January (it has since dropped, closing Friday at $17.06).
Such was the market frenzy on Internet stocks, Cyberian's price rose 74 percent in a single day in November on trades of 9 million shares -- 29 times its average volume.
Yet industry experts say revenue from electronic retail operations such as Cyberian's will be dwarfed by profits made from helping companies do business on the Internet.
Connecticut is home to a growing number of firms that hope to carve out a lucrative niche by enabling electronic commerce. They range from simple Web design operations to firms that provide software and hardware that allow businesses to take full advantage of the interactive nature of the medium.
"We have a lot of companies here," Kent said. "And they tend to be better known nationally than within the state."
Sonalysts Inc. of Waterford, a longtime defense contractor, branched out into movie production and the development of military gaming software. The company is to design Web and intranet applications for firms such as Otis Elevator and the Hartford Financial Services Group.
Modem Media, now Modem Media.Poppe Tyson of Westport after a merger last spring, has emerged as one of the most influential of new Internet marketing firms, and has filed to become a public company.
Corporate Information Technologies of Farmington has developed products that will eventually allow the sale of group health plans over the Internet.
Firms such as Merlot Communications, Business Data Services of Glastonbury, EC Cubed of Wilton and Sirocco Systems sell services or products they say will make the use of the Internet more efficient.
While those and other companies are rushing to provide cutting-edge tools for businesses, Texas-based Electronic Data Systems Corp. has been tentatively selected to upgrade the state's computer systems.
