"To get my 500th wicket was a bonus and to get 10 wickets as well - well, I could not have written the script better for myself," he told reporters.
Warne, starting the final day of the first Test on 496 wickets, ripped through Sri Lanka's top order in the morning, taking three wickets in 15 balls, and then returned in the afternoon to reach the 500-mark with the second ball of his second spell as Hashan Tillakaratne skied a catch to mid-wicket.
The 34-year-old finished with 5 for 43 and match figures of 10 for 159 in his first Test following a 12-month ban for taking a diuretic.
"I thought I got better and better. I was very happy with the way it came out today. I don't think I could have bowled much better," Warne added.
"To come out and do what I did today and win a Test match was a very big achievement...I feel very proud."
"I wouldn't have been able to get there without the faith of Ricky Ponting, who got me in straight away and bowled me a lot of overs, and the support of all the players over the last 12 months."
